@startuml Telegram Notifications

title Telegram Alerts — Account Linking and Notification Delivery

actor Member as U
participant "Browser\n(AccountSettings → Notifications)" as B
participant "TelegramApiServlet\n/api/telegram/*" as API
participant "TelegramWebhookServlet\nPOST /api/telegram/webhook" as WH
participant "TelegramLinkDAO" as LDAO
participant "NotificationService\n(single funnel)" as NS
participant "TelegramNotifier" as TN
participant "TelegramOutboxDAO" as ODAO
participant "TelegramOutboxWorker\n(daemon, every 10s)" as W
participant "TelegramClient" as TC
database "PostgreSQL" as DB
cloud "Telegram Bot API" as TG

== Feature gate ==
note right of API
  <b>TelegramConfig.isConfigured()</b>: bot token +
  bot username + webhook secret all present.
  Unset (local dev) → UI reports unavailable and
  nothing is queued or sent. Mirrors MailConfig.
end note

== Connect: mint one code, two ways to redeem it ==
U -> B : Account settings → Connect Telegram\n(accept PDPA notice)
B -> API : POST /api/telegram/link/start\n(JSESSIONID)
activate API
API -> API : userId = session\n(no client-supplied identity)
API -> LDAO : mintCodes(userId, deepLinkToken, otp)
activate LDAO
note right of LDAO
  Both rows are hashed (SHA-256) before
  storage and share one expiry. The deep
  link and the 6-digit OTP are two
  presentations of the same grant.
end note
LDAO -> DB : INSERT telegram_link_codes\n(kind='DEEPLINK'), (kind='OTP')
deactivate LDAO
API -> B : { deepLink: t.me/<bot>?start=<token>,\n  otp: 123456, expiresAt }
deactivate API
B -> U : Show "Open Telegram" button + code

alt Deep link (tap)
    U -> TG : Opens bot, Telegram sends /start <token>
else Manual OTP (paste)
    U -> TG : Sends 123456 to the bot
end

== Webhook: both paths converge on one single-use consumption ==
TG -> WH : POST /api/telegram/webhook\nX-Telegram-Bot-Api-Secret-Token
activate WH
alt secret header missing or wrong
    note right of WH
      Constant-time compare against
      TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET. Anyone can
      guess the URL; only Telegram has this.
    end note
    WH -> TG : 401 (nothing parsed, nothing logged)
end
WH -> WH : body <= 64 KB, parse update\nTelegramAttemptLimiter: per-chat throttle
WH -> LDAO : consumeCode(rawCode)
activate LDAO
note right of LDAO
  <b>Single use is enforced in SQL, not Java:</b>
  UPDATE telegram_link_codes
     SET used_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
   WHERE code_hash = ? AND used_at IS NULL
     AND expires_at > CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
  RETURNING user_id
  One row updated → the caller owns the grant.
  A replay or a race gets no row.
end note
LDAO -> DB : UPDATE … RETURNING user_id
alt no row (expired, already used, or wrong)
    LDAO --> WH : null
    WH -> TG : reply telegram.bot.invalidCode
end
LDAO --> WH : userId
deactivate LDAO
WH -> LDAO : link(userId, chatId, telegramUsername)
activate LDAO
note right of LDAO
  <b>PDPA</b>: the chat id is never stored raw.
  chat_id_hash      = SHA-256(chatId + pepper) — searchable
  chat_id_encrypted = AES-256-GCM — reversible to send
  linked_at doubles as the consent timestamp.
  Partial unique indexes keep one active link
  per user and per chat, so re-linking is safe.
end note
LDAO -> DB : deactivate prior link, INSERT telegram_links
LDAO --> WH : LinkOutcome(LINKED / MOVED)
deactivate LDAO
alt link moved to a different chat
    WH -> TG : tell the displaced chat (telegram.bot.moved)
end
WH -> TG : reply telegram.bot.linked
deactivate WH

== Notification: one preference decision, three channels ==
note over NS
  Every event enters through NotificationService.create.
  In-app, email and Telegram are decided <b>once</b>, here,
  so a channel cannot drift from the others and the legacy
  JSP bid path gets Telegram without knowing it exists.
end note

U -> NS : bid placed / auction concluded\n(BidApiServlet, OrderApiServlet,\nAuctionFinalizer, PlaceBidServlet)
activate NS
NS -> DB : SELECT title, price, bid count, date_end
alt event type is opted out (allowedByPreference)
    NS -> NS : return — suppressed in all three channels
end
NS -> DB : INSERT INTO notifications  (in-app)
NS -> NS : send email if MailConfig.isSmtpConfigured()
NS -> TN : enqueue(userId, TelegramAlerts.Alert)
activate TN
note right of TN
  Three gates, cheapest first:
  1. TelegramConfig.isConfigured()
  2. user has an active link
  3. per-event column on notification_preference
     (telegram_seller_price defaults FALSE —
      the price feed is opt-in)
end note
alt not configured, not linked, or opted out
    TN --> NS : return (nothing queued)
end
TN -> ODAO : enqueue(…, dedupeKey, initialDelaySeconds)
activate ODAO
note right of ODAO
  <b>Coalescing.</b> ON CONFLICT names the partial
  unique index on dedupe_key WHERE status='PENDING',
  and DO UPDATE rewrites <b>body only</b>.
  PRICE:{auctionId} is inserted due one cooldown
  ahead, so bids arriving inside that window
  refresh the figure instead of adding messages —
  and because next_attempt_at is never touched on
  conflict, continuous bidding cannot starve it.
  RESULT:{auctionId} lets the four conclusion
  paths and lazy re-entry all try, and send once.
end note
ODAO -> DB : INSERT … ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE SET body
deactivate ODAO
deactivate TN
NS --> U : primary action already returned;\nnotification failures never propagate
deactivate NS

== Delivery: the worker owns every way this can go wrong ==
W -> ODAO : claimDue(20)
activate ODAO
note right of ODAO
  One UPDATE … RETURNING that pushes
  next_attempt_at 120s out as it hands rows
  over: a lease, not a held lock. A worker
  that dies mid-batch strands nothing.
  FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED keeps two
  instances off the same row.
end note
ODAO -> DB : UPDATE … WHERE status='PENDING'\nAND next_attempt_at <= now()\nFOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED RETURNING …
ODAO --> W : up to 20 PendingMessage
deactivate ODAO

loop each message (~1100ms apart, ≈1 msg/s/chat)
    W -> DB : find active link → decrypt chat id
    alt link gone since enqueue
        W -> ODAO : markSkipped("no active Telegram link")
    end
    W -> TC : sendMessage(chatId, body)
    TC -> TG : POST /bot<token>/sendMessage\nparse_mode=HTML
    alt 200 OK
        TG --> TC : ok
        W -> ODAO : markSent — status='SENT', sent_at
    else 429 Too Many Requests
        TG --> TC : retry_after = N
        note right of W
          Flood control is <b>our pacing</b> being wrong,
          not the message being undeliverable, so
          delayFor(N) moves next_attempt_at and
          leaves attempts alone — the row keeps
          its full retry budget. N clamped to 300s.
        end note
        W -> ODAO : delayFor(id, N)
    else 403, or 400 "chat not found"
        TG --> TC : bot blocked / chat gone
        note right of W
          Retrying can never help. The link is
          deactivated so nothing else is queued
          for this member, and the row is SKIPPED
          — distinct from FAILED, which means
          "we ran out of retries" and is worth
          an operator's attention.
        end note
        W -> LDAO : unlinkUser(userId)
        W -> ODAO : markSkipped(403 …)
    else other 4xx
        W -> ODAO : markSkipped — malformed, repetition won't fix it
    else 5xx / timeout
        note right of W
          Backoff ladder 10s, 30s, 2m, 10m, 30m,
          then FAILED at 5 attempts with the last
          error kept. Front-loaded because most
          failures are a transient edge 502.
        end note
        W -> ODAO : markFailed(id, attempts, error)
    end
end

note over U, TG
  <b>Privacy (PDPA) enforced by signature, not by memory.</b>
  TelegramAlerts.outbid / lost / sellerPrice take no user id or username
  at all, so naming the other party would require changing a signature a
  reviewer would notice. sellerSold is the one bounded exception — the
  seller is about to ship to this person — and it masks the handle
  (c***e) <i>inside</i> the builder, so no call site can skip it. Titles
  are user input and every substitution is HTML-escaped before markup is
  added around it. The bot token appears in no log, error or stored row.
end note

@enduml
